  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nBroad high pressure centered south of Hudson Bay will slide east\nthrough mid week. Latest satellite data shows ample cloud cover\nacross the upper Ohio Valley associated with a subtle short wave\ntracking east. The 12Z model suite is in good agreement that\nthis cloud cover will overspread the area tonight leading to\nmostly cloudy skies. This will limit radiational cooling while\nsome patchy frost across the north and valley fog cannot be\nruled out tonight. Lows tonight will range from the mid 30s\nnorth to mid 40s south. Skies will transition to mostly clear\nTuesday with highs mainly in the 60s. While skies look to remain\nmostly clear Tuesday night northeast flow will be on the\nincrease and could hamper radiational cooling. This has the\npotential to limit frost across remaining active growing zones\nsouth of the mountains. Wednesday will be similar to Tuesday \nwith mostly sunny skies and highs in the 60s. Winds and skies\nmay be more favorable for radiational cooling Wednesday night\nthat could lead to frost in active growing zones south of the\nmountains.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn anomalously strong high pressure center builds across \nEastern Canada and New England on Wednesday and Thursday. This \nbrings with it mainly sunny skies and seasonably cool \nconditions. \n\nAt the same time, a coastal storm begins to organize off the \nCarolinas by Wednesday, and continue to organize on Thursday. \nThe high pressure center will keep the storm well to our south \nthrough at least Thursday. The one impact we'll start to notice \nfrom this system will be building surf starting by Wednesday. \nThe interaction between the high pressure center and the \ndeveloping storm produces an expansive easterly fetch across the\nNorth Atlantic, with surf building on Wednesday and Thursday. A\nnortheasterly flow will also become more distinguishable along \nthe coast by Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nBy Friday, the ridge of high pressure begins to break down, \nallowing the storm to track further northward toward New \nEngland. Clouds increase through the day, and the northeasterly \nflow steadily strengthens along the coast as well. Seas continue\nto build, with surf approaching 10 feet by late Friday.\n\nThe exact track of the system remains in question, with models \nand ensembles waffling between a track looping near Cape Cod, \ncloser to New Jersey, or mostly remaining out to sea. The track \nhas the biggest implications for rainfall amounts, with these \ndifferent solutions ranging from virtually no rainfall to \nseveral inches. It's worth noting that the most skillful model \nsuites, the Euro and AIFS, show the stronger storm with a closer\ntrack near Cape Cod, which would be the highest impact solution\nfor northern New England. However, these Northwest Atlantic \nLows also tend to be the kind of systems that these models can \nover-amplify with the system. \n\nSo at this point we have time to continue to monitor the model \ntrends, with any land based impacts unlikely to be felt before \nlate Friday. Surf begins building on Wednesday, and is likely \nlarge for Friday through Sunday regardless of the storm track.",
